Great Powers: Russia and Britain.

The small expedition party, travelling in extreme climatic conditions, suffered extraordinary privations with courage and equanimity. Werner Otto von Hentig was a political leaders from Berlin (1886-05-22, Berlin–1984-08-08). Holds notable historical significance, documented across 0 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to political systems and geopolitics.", "newValue": "Werner Otto von Hentig (1886–1984) was a German Army officer, adventurer, and diplomat whose career spanned two world wars and the nascent Cold War, profoundly influencing German foreign policy in the Middle East and Central Asia. Born in Berlin, his early life was marked by a commission from Kaiser Wilhelm II in 1915 to lead a covert expedition into the volatile territories of Central Asia, a region then at the heart of the "Great Game" between the British and Russian Empires. Hentig's audacious mission aimed to incite an anti-British uprising among Afghan tribes and challenge British dominance in India, establishing a German presence in a strategically crucial but largely unexplored geopolitical theater.

The Hentig Expedition, launched in April 1915 from Baghdad, was an extraordinary feat of endurance and clandestine diplomacy. Comprising a small group of German and Turkish officers, it traversed the treacherous deserts and mountains of Persia and Afghanistan under extreme climatic conditions, suffering significant privations. Their objective was to reach Kabul and forge an alliance with Emir Habibullah Khan of Afghanistan, promising German military aid and support for Afghan independence in exchange for a declaration of war against British India. While the expedition successfully reached Kabul in September 1915, the mission ultimately failed to secure Habibullah's full commitment to war, though it did exert considerable diplomatic pressure on British authorities and highlighted the vulnerability of their colonial holdings."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-29T07:07:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30430149831", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Modern era: industrialization, imperial expansion, and democratic revolutions", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1886-05-22, Berlin"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1886-05-22, Berlin"}], "newValue": ["Kaiser Wilhelm II's strategic ambition to destabilize British India led to the commissioning of the Hentig Expedition in 1915.", "The ongoing "Great Game" rivalry between the British and Russian Empires created a geopolitical vacuum in Central Asia that Germany sought to exploit.", "The Ottoman Empire's entry into World War I provided Germany with a crucial land route through allied territory to reach Central Asia."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-29T07:07:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30430149831", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1984-08-08"}, {"title": "Contributed to mass society, world wars, and foundations of conte